In article <CIrK97.MKp@mcm.hp.com> marco@hpmcaa.mcm.hp.com (Marco Dalla-Gasperina) writes:

>Well back to nethack... how about having a Pandora's box?  Opening
>it can have all sorts of nasties after you.

Now, *this* is an interesting idea.  Open it, and in the first turn a bunch
of nasties pop out.  But "Hope" doesn't pop out until the second turn --
what's hope?  Well.

Perhaps it's an unusually powerful monster -- roughly Archon-power -- which, if 
you just let it out, will be "friendly" -- slightly better than "peaceful," but 
not "tame."  I would define this as "it tends to hang around with you, follow 
you up and down levels, and get between you and nasties" -- but it doesn't fight
for you, and won't fetch for you.

*BUT*: if you put the lid on after the nasties pop out, then "Hope" will ask to
be let out; and if you let it out, it's tame.

Actually, something that fights for you is pretty lame.  "Hope" should be 
somehow unique.

**********

I kinda like this idea.  Perhaps some other boxes are in order -- incidentally,
this is a bit of a trivia quiz; who can identify the sources of the most of 
these?  *EMAIL* results only

Musical box -- it's basically a small musical instrument.  It plays the tune for
the Castle gate.  But! there is a 90% chance when you play it that the Ghost of 
Henry -- who is a "special," has [in|suc]cubus "seduction" power, *plus* his 
touch is an automatic drain of 1-3 levels.  Each time he drains, Henry 
"appears older."  But he can be dispelled by a special use of the box. . .  
which destroys it.

Cardboard Box -- there is a very small percent chance that it is the material
plane manifestation of a Lord of Order, which will spew out oracularities at 
random moments; otherwise it's useless (too weak to carry anything in).  
Obviously, useful only to Lawfuls, so we need balancing objects for Chaotics and
Neutrals.

Blue box -- this is the box for Neutrals.  Like the Cardboard Box, if it works
(same chance as Cardboard Box) it will at random times open up a communication
channel to a Lord of Balance, who will spew out an oracularity.  Will happen 
more often, but there is a smaller chance that it will be valuable (i.e., it 
will have a chance of being a Lesser, and therefore possibly false, 
oracularity).

Johann Sebastian Box -- the box for Chaotics (who, as we all know, love puns 
with an unholy passion).  *IF* it is the manifestation of a Lord of Chaos 
(again, same chance as the other two), it spews out oracularities fairly often,
but they're almost always Lesser; however, it has a very small chance of 
revealing The Tune.  Obviously, there's no special reference in this one, having
forgone that pleasure in favor of the pun.

Black Box -- absorbs 50% of damage from heat- and cold-based attacks.  After a
heat-based attack, acts as a lantern for # of turns equal to the points of
damage it absorbed.

Fox socks Knox box -- opening it confuses you, but there is a small chance of
getting a fox as a pet, and a small chance of it increasing your wisdom.  (The
two chances are mutually exclusive.)  After one opening it disappears, but it
reappears elsewhere in the dungeon.

Box of Rain -- (A)pplying it allows you to tame undead.

Box of Pain -- (A)pplying it causes you intense agony, but no damage, for 1d10
turns, during which you are paralyzed.  At the end of the period, if you were
polymorphed or lycanthropic, you are returned to human -- or you become one,
permanently, if you were an elf! -- and you are now resistant to lycanthropy 
and polymorphing.  Generally carried by co-aligned priestesses who offer to 
apply it to you.  (If you refuse, there is a 10% chance they will attack.)
Also, your wisdom goes up 1d3-1.  Only one exists, and it may be applied only
once.

Jack-in-the-Box -- May be applied as many times as there are charges.  Each 
time, there is a 10% chance you will be transported -- *EVEN*IF*YOU*HAVE*THE*
AMULET* -- to the delicatessen on the Gnomish town level.  (This is the
"mystery reference" for this one.)  Otherwise, it produces a Kid's Meal.  10% 
chance the Kid's Meal will make you sick; it kept, it rots *very* fast.



